<p>One of the most frustrating parts of Achilles tendinopathy — more commonly called Achilles tendonitis — is that progress doesn't feel the way you'd expect.</p>

<p>You're doing your exercises. You've backed off the activities that aggravated it. But some mornings it still stiffens up, and you're not sure if you're actually getting better or just managing symptoms.</p>

<p>I have this conversation with patients regularly. Recovery from Achilles tendinopathy is often slow, non-linear, and hard to read — especially if you don't know what to look for. So I want to walk you through the real signs that things are heading in the right direction.</p>

<h2>Why Recovery Is Hard to Gauge</h2>

<p>Tendons don't behave like muscles. When a muscle is sore from a hard workout, you feel it the next day and then it fades. Tendons have a delayed irritability response — symptoms can spike 24 hours after the provocative activity, not immediately. This makes it genuinely difficult to connect cause and effect.</p>

<p>The other issue is that tendon structure and tendon pain don't always track together. You can have structural changes visible on ultrasound with very little pain, and you can have significant pain with relatively minor structural findings. Relying on how it "feels" in the moment isn't always reliable.</p>

<p>What we want to track is a cluster of indicators over time — not just today's pain level.</p>

<h2>Sign 1: Morning Stiffness Is Resolving Faster</h2>

<p>Morning stiffness is one of the cardinal symptoms of Achilles tendinopathy. Most people describe needing to "walk it off" for 10–30 minutes before the tendon feels normal.</p>

<p>As recovery progresses, this stiffness should ease more quickly — from 20 minutes down to 10, then down to a few minutes, then largely gone. Track how long it takes each morning. It's a simple, consistent daily measure.</p>

<p>If your morning stiffness has gone from 20 minutes to 5 minutes over the past month, that's a meaningful signal — even if you still have some discomfort during activity.</p>

<h2>Sign 2: Pain During Activity Stays Below 4 Out of 10</h2>

<p>The pain monitoring system I use in my programs is adapted from a simple 0–10 scale, where 0 is no pain and 10 is the worst pain imaginable. During your rehab exercises and activity, the target is to stay below 4/10.</p>

<p>Early in recovery, you might be hitting 5 or 6 during heavier loading. A sign of progress is that the same exercises that used to push you to 5/10 now sit at 2–3/10. You're tolerating the same or greater load with less provocation.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>Pain is information, not damage. A little discomfort during your exercises is expected — it's the trend over weeks that tells the real story.</p>
</blockquote>

<h2>Sign 3: 24-Hour Response Is Settling</h2>

<p>This is one of the most important markers I track with patients. After any loading session or increased activity, how do you feel the next morning?</p>

<p>Early in rehab, you might wake up significantly stiffer or more painful after a big day. As the tendon adapts, the 24-hour response should diminish. The same amount of activity should produce less next-day reaction over time.</p>

<p>Here's a practical test: after your loading session, rate your pain the next morning compared to a normal morning. If that gap is closing — if your "loaded morning" is feeling closer to your "normal morning" — you're building tendon capacity.</p>

<h2>Sign 4: You Can Load More Than You Could Before</h2>

<p>This one is straightforward but often overlooked. Are you actually progressing your exercises?</p>

<p>If you started with bodyweight double-leg calf raises and you're now doing single-leg raises with added weight, that's objective evidence that the tendon is tolerating more. That's adaptation.</p>

<p>If you've been doing the same exercise at the same load for six weeks without progression, it may mean the tendon is holding steady but not actively building capacity — worth reviewing with a clinician.</p>

<h2>Sign 5: Less Tenderness to Touch</h2>

<p>Find the most tender point on your Achilles tendon — usually a focal spot along the tendon body or at the insertion. Press it with your thumb. Rate the tenderness on a scale of 0–10.</p>

<p>This tenderness should gradually decrease over the course of your recovery. It won't disappear overnight, but comparing it month to month gives you a concrete marker that doesn't rely on memory or subjective impression.</p>

<p>For mid-portion tendinopathy, the tender spot is usually 2–6 cm above the heel. For insertional, it's right at the heel bone attachment.</p>

<h2>Sign 6: You Can Do More Activity Without Flares</h2>

<p>Can you walk further, stand longer, or return to light jogging that used to provoke your symptoms? Activity tolerance is the real-world measure of recovery.</p>

<p>Track your functional capacity: what you can do now versus what you could do 6 weeks ago. If you went from struggling with a 10-minute walk to managing 30 minutes without a flare, that's meaningful progress — even if the tendon isn't symptom-free.</p>

<h2>What Doesn't Mean You're Getting Better</h2>

<p>A few things can feel like progress but aren't reliable indicators:</p>

<p><strong>Pain-free on rest.</strong> Tendons can feel completely fine when you're sitting down, even when they're not recovering well. Pain with loading is the relevant signal.</p>

<p><strong>One good day.</strong> Tendon recovery isn't linear. You'll have good days and setback days. What matters is the weekly trend, not the daily read.</p>

<p><strong>Pain disappears quickly with stretching or massage.</strong> Symptom relief and actual recovery are different things. If your pain reliably disappears with a calf rub but returns with activity, the underlying tendon capacity hasn't changed.</p>

<h2>How Long Should This Take?</h2>

<p>I'll be direct: most Achilles tendinopathy cases take 3–6 months of consistent, well-dosed rehabilitation to reach meaningful functional recovery. Some take longer. The timeline depends on how irritable the tendon is, how long the problem has been present, your training load, and a number of individual factors.</p>

<p>The goal in the early weeks isn't to be pain-free — it's to <em>stabilize</em> the tendon so you can begin progressive loading. The loading phase is where the real recovery happens. That takes time, and that's normal.</p>

<p>If you're three months in without clear progress on the signs above, it's worth getting a thorough assessment. Some people need their program modified; others have a contributing factor that hasn't been addressed.</p>

<h2>Track Your Progress — Not Just Your Pain</h2>

<p>I encourage every patient to keep a simple log: morning stiffness duration, pain during exercise (0–10), 24-hour response, and what activities they could manage. Even a rough weekly note is more useful than relying on memory.</p>

<p>Progress in Achilles tendinopathy can feel invisible week to week, but when you look back over a month or two, the trend is usually clear. That perspective is what keeps people on track when a bad day makes it feel like nothing is working.</p>

